re: Consider breaking down the query into smaller, more manageable pieces using subqueries or CTEs (Common Table Expressions).\n5. Optimize the query for performance: The original query may not be optimized for performance, so consider rewriting it to use indexes, join types, and other optimization techniques.\n\nHere's a rewritten version of the SQL query that breaks down some of these steps:\n```sql\nWITH \n  -- Step 1: Identify main tables and relationships\n  sales_data AS (\n    SELECT \n      s_item_sk,\n      cs_ext_list_price,\n      SUM(cr_refunded_cash) AS refund_amount,\n      COUNT(*) AS sale_count\n    FROM \n      store_sales\n    JOIN \n      catalog_sales ON s_item_sk = cs_item_sk\n    JOIN \n      date_dim ON s_sold_date_sk = d_date_sk\n    WHERE \n      cs_ext_list_price > 2 * (SELECT AVG(cs_ext_list_price) FROM store_sales)\n    GROUP BY \n      s_item_sk, cs_ext_list_price\n  ),\n  \n  -- Step 2: Filter and aggregate data\n  filtered_data AS (\n    SELECT \n      *\n    FROM \n      sales_data\n    WHERE \n      refund_amount > 0 AND sale_count > 10\n    GROUP BY \n      s_item_sk, cs_ext_list_price\n  )\n\n-- Step 3: Join and filter final results\nSELECT \n  i_product_name,\n  i_item_sk,\n  s_store_name,\n  s_zip,\n  ca_street_number,\n  ca_street_name,\n  ca_city,\n  ca_zip,\n  d_year,\n  COUNT(*) AS cnt\nFROM \n  filtered_data\nJOIN \n  item ON s_item_sk = i_item_sk\nJOIN \n  store ON s_store_sk = s_store_sk\nJOIN \n  customer_address ON ca_address_sk = s_addr_sk\nWHERE \n  -- Additional filters and conditions go here\nGROUP BY \n  i_product_name, i_item_sk, s_store_name, s_zip, ca_street_number, ca_street_name, ca_city, ca_zip, d_year;\n```\nNote that this is just one possible way to rewrite the SQL query. The actual rewritten query may depend on the specific requirements and constraints of your database schema and data.", 'time': 13.320268154144287}
