can(table=[[customer]])\n                                    LogicalTableScan(table=[[customer_demographics]])\n                                  LogicalTableScan(table=[[customer_demographics]])\n                                LogicalTableScan(table=[[promotion]])\n                              LogicalTableScan(table=[[household_demographics]])\n                            LogicalTableScan(table=[[household_demographics]])\n                          LogicalTableScan(table=[[customer_address]])\n                        LogicalTableScan(table=[[customer_address]])\n                      LogicalTableScan(table=[[income_band]])\n                    LogicalTableScan(table=[[income_band]])\n                  LogicalTableScan(table=[[item]])\n  \n```"}], 'response': "The SQL query is a complex query that involves multiple joins and filters. The query appears to be analyzing sales data from various tables, including `store_sales`, `catalog_sales`, and `date_dim`. The query also includes filters based on the wholesale cost of items, marital status, education level, and other demographic information.\n\nThe query can be rewritten using a SQL query rewrite rule to improve its performance. Here are some suggestions:\n\n1. Use indexes: Create indexes on columns that are used in the WHERE clause or JOIN conditions.\n2. Simplify joins: Instead of using multiple inner joins, consider using outer joins or subqueries to simplify the query.\n3. Filter early: Apply filters as early as possible in the query to reduce the amount of data that needs to be processed.\n4. Use efficient aggregate functions: Replace SUM() with AVG() if you're looking for an average value.\n\nHere's a rewritten version of the query:\n```sql\nWITH sales AS (\n  SELECT \n    ss_item_sk, \n    ss_sold_date_sk, \n    ss_customer_sk, \n    ss_cdemo_sk, \n    ss_hdemo_sk, \n    ss_addr_sk, \n    ss_promo_sk, \n    SUM(ss_wholesale_cost) AS total_cost\n  FROM store_sales\n  WHERE \n    ss_item_sk IN (\n      SELECT i_item_sk\n      FROM item\n      WHERE i_current_price BETWEEN 80 AND 90\n    )\n    AND \n    ss_customer_sk IN (\n      SELECT c_customer_sk\n      FROM customer\n      WHERE \n        c_marital_status <> 'M'\n        AND c_education_status IN ('Unknown', '2 yr Degree')\n    )\n  GROUP BY \n    ss_item_sk, \n    ss_sold_date_sk, \n    ss_customer_sk, \n    ss_cdemo_sk, \n    ss_hdemo_sk, \n    ss_addr_sk, \n    ss_promo_sk\n),\ncatalog_sales AS (\n  SELECT \n    cs_item_sk, \n    cs_ext_list_price, \n    SUM(cr_refunded_cash) AS total_refund\n  FROM catalog_sales\n  JOIN catalog_returns ON cr_item_sk = cs_item_sk\n  WHERE \n    cs_item_sk IN (\n      SELECT i_item_sk\n      FROM item\n      WHERE i_current_price BETWEEN 80 AND 90\n    )\n  GROUP BY \n    cs_item_sk, \n    cs_ext_list_price\n)\nSELECT \n  s.ss_item_sk, \n  s.ss_sold_date_sk, \n  s.ss_customer_sk, \n  s.ss_cdemo_sk, \n  s.ss_hdemo_sk, \n  s.ss_addr_sk, \n  s.ss_promo_sk, \n  s.total_cost, \n  c.cs_ext_list_price, \n  c.total_refund\nFROM sales s\nJOIN catalog_sales c ON s.ss_item_sk = c.cs_item_sk\nWHERE \n  s.total_cost > (2 * c.cs_ext_list_price)\n  AND \n  s.total_cost BETWEEN 73 AND 93;\n```\nThis rewritten query uses subqueries to simplify the joins and filters, and applies filters early in the query. It also uses efficient aggregate functions like SUM() instead of COUNT().", 'time': 28.560325145721436}
